<h2 id="workspace-layout">Choose a comfortable workspace layout</h2>
<p>The seven public writing and conversion tools start with <strong>Full-width panels</strong>. The editor comes first, then the reading preview and export controls. Previews use the page's scrolling, so long articles do not need a second scrolling area inside the page. The three-step bar brings review and export into view without changing the writing.</p>
<p>Choose <strong>Side by side on wide screens</strong> under Workspace layout to compare the editor and reading preview. On screens up to 850 pixels wide, either choice uses one column and previews remain in the page flow. In a wide comparison layout, focus the reading preview to scroll its bounded reading area with a keyboard.</p>
<p><strong>Edit article</strong> in the step bar stays nearby while you read. <strong>Back to editing</strong> is also available below the reading preview and at the start of the export controls once an article is present. These shortcuts focus the article body and keep its cursor or selected passage. They do not reopen the Word picker or Google Docs paste box. A pending article import takes precedence: apply or clear it first. File operations temporarily disable these shortcuts.</p>
<p>Layout changes leave article fields, private notes, selected text, checkpoints, and export approval as they are. Editing writing still clears approval. The layout choice lasts only in the open tab and is excluded from backups and exports. Reopening the tool starts with full-width panels. There is no autosave.</p>
<h2 id="guided-workflow">Move from writing to review to export</h2>
<p>The three-step bar stays nearby as you move through the workspace. Add writing opens your input; Word and Google Docs tools offer their own file or paste starting point. Review opens the reading preview and brings it into view. Export takes you to destination choices and the separate review checkbox. The highlighted step records navigation, not completion or a quality score.</p>
<p>After adding an article, choose <strong>Review this article</strong> below the editor, then <strong>Continue to WordPress</strong>, <strong>Continue to HTML source</strong>, or <strong>Continue to Word</strong> below the preview. An unfinished article import takes precedence in these shortcuts: inspect its pending conversion and apply or clear it before using them for the current article. Navigation keeps writing, source notes, selections, export choices, and approval. It never copies, downloads, or publishes.</p>
<p>The bar reports pending imports, file operations, and your current approval choice. Typing changes no focus or scroll position automatically. File-first conversion previews and explicit navigation bring the relevant section into view. The bar becomes static in short windows so it takes less space while you work. The navigation state stays in this tab and is excluded from backups and exports. Save a private backup before leaving.</p>
